=== Annual events ===
Main festivals <ref>{{Cite web|Japanese book|url=https://niukawakami-jinja.jp/annual-events/|title=Festivals/Annual Events|accessdate=2021.11.9|publisher=Nyukawakami Shrine}}</ref>.
*January 16th - Suesha Mizu Shrine Festival
*April 16th - Suesha Kirai Shrine Annual Festival
*April 17th - Suesha Toshogu Shrine Festival
*June 4th - Water God Festival. People from companies involved in water, such as hydroelectric power generation and water supply projects, will be in attendance.
*July 16th - Setsusha Nyu Shrine annual festival.
*Second Sunday of October - Ogawa Festival (Taikodai Hosho Safety Prayer Festival) also known as the "Fighting Festival". Previously, it was held on {{ill|lt=September 16th (lunar calendar)|Q840252|WD=Q840252|lt=September 16th of the lunar calendar|1=September 16 (Lunisolar)}}, but it was changed in 1922. Also known as the ``Danjiri Festival,'' eight taiko drum stands are brought out from within the shrine ward and dance wildly around the shrine grounds. It is one of the three major festivals of Yamato.
*October 16th - Annual festival.

=== Cultural assets ===

=== Important cultural property (designated by the country) ===
* '''Stone lantern''' - Designated date: {{ill|lt=1963|Q2755|WD=Q2755|1=1963}} (1963) February 14th<ref>{{Cite web|Japanese book|url=https://kunishitei.bunka.go.jp/heritage/detail/201/6848|title=Stone lantern/Nationally designated cultural property database|accessdate=2021-11-08|publisher=Agency for Cultural Affairs}}</ref>.

=== Natural Monument (Nationally Designated) ===* '''{{ill|lt=Nyukawa Kaminakasha Shrine Natural Area|Q121038166|WD=Q121038166|1=Niukawakaminakasha no Tsurumanryo jiseichi}}''' - Designated date: {{ill|lt=1957|Q5311|WD=Q5311|1=1957}} (Showa 32) May 8th<ref>{{Cite web|Japanese book|url=https://kunishitei.bunka.go.jp/heritage/detail/401/1997|title=Nyukawakamichusha's natural habitat of the crane manryo/Nationally designated cultural properties database|accessdate=2021-11-08|publisher=Agency for Cultural Affairs}}</ref>. It grows in clusters on Mt. Komurodake behind the main shrine. {{ill|lt=Evergreen forest|Q1024867|WD=Q1024867|1=Evergreen forest}} An evergreen stolonous shrub belonging to the family {{ill|lt=Aperaceae|Q161260|WD=Q161260|1=Myrsinaceae}} that grows on the bright forest floor of {{ill|lt=evergreen forests|Q1024867|WD=Q1024867|1=Evergreen forest}}. {{ill|lt=dioecious|Q148681|WD=Q148681|1=Dioecy}}. The stems grow straight at first and then slender. The long ones reach 1.5 meters, the flowers open around June-July, and the fruits become red-hot in late autumn-winter. It grows in clusters on semi-shady slopes in evergreen forests such as {{ill|lt=Chii|Q1065400|WD=Q1065400|1=Castanopsis}} and {{ill|lt=Sakaki|Q775470|WD=Q775470|1=Cleyera japonica}}. It succumbs to other weeds in bright, fertile areas, and declines in dark areas. Seedlings are rarely seen, and they are mainly propagated by stolons.

=== Cultural property designated by Nara Prefecture ===
* '''Wooden Seated Statue of Elephant Goddess, Seated Statue of Male God, Seated Statue of Goddess, Seated Statue of Baby God''' 20 pieces - Designated date: {{ill|lt=2018|Q25291|WD=Q25291|1=2018}} February 2, 2018<ref>{{Cite web|Japanese book|url=https://www.pref.nara.jp/secure/143530/kenshiteiichiran.pdf|title=List of cultural properties designated by Nara Prefecture|accessdate=2021-11-08|publisher=Nara Prefecture|page=13|format=PDF}}</ref>. One of the statues is a ``Seated Statue of the Elephant Goddess,'' which is 54 centimeters tall and made of {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=one piece of wood|1=UNKNOWN}}, and is said to be the oldest statue in Japan dressed in Japanese clothing. In addition, two statues of goddesses in Chinese robes and four statues of male gods were enshrined, both of which are said to have been created in the late {{ill|lt=History of Japanese Art#Heian Period|Q11508877|WD=Q11508877|lt=Fujiwara Period|1=history of Japanese art}}.

